WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.140
Authentication identifies the caller; authorization decides what records and fields the caller may access or modify.

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:11.960
Prisma does not automatically know the application's business authorization rules.

3
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:21.900
Every protected read, create, update, delete, export, aggregation, or administrative operation needs server-side authorization.

4
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:31.840
A route that checks whether a user is logged in may still be unsafe if it forgets record-level ownership, tenant context, role requirements, or field-level restrictions.

5
00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:38.920
Object-level authorization should be enforced close to the data operation or in a shared service that every relevant path uses.

6
00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:46.620
If a user requests an invoice, document, or project by ID, the Prisma query should not search only by ID.

7
00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:54.160
It should also enforce the organization, tenant, ownership, or role context that makes access legitimate.

8
00:00:54.260 --> 00:01:04.500
Multi-tenant applications should derive tenant boundaries server-side and include them consistently in reads, writes, deletes, exports, and background jobs.

9
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:09.640
Do not trust client-supplied tenant IDs as proof of access.

10
00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.800
Field exposure needs intentional design.

11
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:17.860
Prisma select and include are powerful, but they can also return more than the caller needs.

12
00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:23.640
Avoid returning raw Prisma model objects directly when they contain internal or sensitive fields.

13
00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:38.700
Password hashes, reset tokens, API keys, internal notes, security flags, billing details, audit-only fields, and privileged relationships should be excluded unless the use case explicitly requires them.

14
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:47.040
Admin tools, support workflows, background jobs, and exports need authorization models too, because internal does not mean harmless.
